WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:31.000
A.

00:50.640 --> 00:51.640
It was a peaceful evening.

00:52.840 --> 00:58.160
I never suspected Inspector Lestrade would shortly be accusing me of the murder of a

00:58.160 --> 01:00.160
man I had yet to meet.

01:05.160 --> 01:07.160
Do you like Borshark?

01:07.160 --> 01:10.160
Yes, you must play him sometime.

01:12.160 --> 01:14.160
I have been.

01:19.160 --> 01:21.160
Do you care to go out?

01:21.160 --> 01:22.160
Why?

01:22.160 --> 01:25.160
I know it's a walk somewhere. I feel restless.

01:25.160 --> 01:28.160
Why don't you read, Holmes?

01:29.160 --> 01:31.160
What, about the tigers of India?

01:31.160 --> 01:35.160
It's a jolly instinct, especially about the female of the species.

01:36.160 --> 01:38.160
Female of the species usually is.

01:39.160 --> 01:45.160
It says here that they often attack when you think they're going to run and run when you think they're going to attack.

01:45.160 --> 01:46.160
Fascinating.

01:46.160 --> 01:47.160
Here.

01:47.160 --> 01:52.160
Now come, come, the exercise will do you good. You must allow yourself to vegetate and grow static.

01:52.160 --> 01:57.160
This little man of your former violent habits. Tiger hunting in India, horse marches, Watson.

01:57.160 --> 01:58.160
Come on.

01:58.160 --> 02:00.160
All right, dear. All right.

02:01.160 --> 02:03.160
But only a short one, mind.

02:03.160 --> 02:08.160
For this is no time to go promenading blast.

02:09.160 --> 02:11.160
There you are, boy.

02:12.160 --> 02:15.160
Well, look. This is my carriage.

02:15.160 --> 02:17.160
You'd think I'd keep it, but it just isn't too small.

02:17.160 --> 02:18.160
Well, yes.

02:18.160 --> 02:21.160
Well, you'd probably pick someone else up, wouldn't you?

02:21.160 --> 02:22.160
But...

02:22.160 --> 02:23.160
No, I...

02:25.160 --> 02:27.160
Look, it's a letter, isn't it?

02:27.160 --> 02:29.160
Maybe it's got an address.

02:29.160 --> 02:31.160
Well, it doesn't make any sense.

02:31.160 --> 02:32.160
Let's see.

02:32.160 --> 02:35.160
Twelve heroes with broken feet.

02:35.160 --> 02:36.160
Unsigned.

02:36.160 --> 02:38.160
What, it sounds silly to me.

02:38.160 --> 02:41.160
Written by a woman, dashed off in a hurry, too.

02:41.160 --> 02:45.160
It is a bold hand, Watson. A woman of strength and character.

02:45.160 --> 02:47.160
Capable of violence, I'd say.

02:47.160 --> 02:51.160
Like your tiger's friend.

02:57.160 --> 02:59.160
Which of you two gentlemen is Dr. Watson?

02:59.160 --> 03:00.160
I am, sir.

03:00.160 --> 03:02.160
Thank goodness I found you, Doctor.

03:02.160 --> 03:05.160
The cloakroom gentlemen of the club tell me this must be your coat.

03:05.160 --> 03:07.160
Ah, yes. I see you have it, Doctor.

03:07.160 --> 03:10.160
As a matter of fact, we've only just discovered there was...

03:10.160 --> 03:12.160
In fact, we were on our way back to the...

03:12.160 --> 03:13.160
I say, would you mind, Holmes?

03:13.160 --> 03:14.160
Yes, of course.

03:14.160 --> 03:16.160
Thank you.

03:21.160 --> 03:22.160
There we are.

03:22.160 --> 03:23.160
Oh!

03:24.160 --> 03:25.160
So sorry.

03:25.160 --> 03:26.160
There we are.

03:27.160 --> 03:29.160
Thank you, Doctor, and thank you.

03:29.160 --> 03:31.160
Good night, gentlemen. I'm sorry about the mistake.

03:31.160 --> 03:32.160
Good night.

03:32.160 --> 03:33.160
Good night.

03:40.160 --> 03:41.160
I say, Holmes.

03:41.160 --> 03:42.160
Yes.

03:42.160 --> 03:43.160
I know.

03:43.160 --> 03:44.160
He took your hat.

03:44.160 --> 03:46.160
Oh, no, now, really, that's gone too far.

03:46.160 --> 03:47.160
There's a limit to these things.

03:47.160 --> 03:48.160
Really, these people.

03:48.160 --> 03:49.160
Oh, don't worry about it, Holmes.

03:49.160 --> 03:52.160
The cloakroom attendants of the club will know his name and address.

03:52.160 --> 03:53.160
Well, it doesn't matter about that.

03:53.160 --> 03:56.160
It's... I don't like wearing another chap's hat, you know.

03:56.160 --> 03:57.160
It's a personal thing.

03:57.160 --> 03:58.160
I have, I...

03:58.160 --> 04:00.160
I look like a toothbrush.

04:08.160 --> 04:10.160
The son of a title, I see.

04:10.160 --> 04:12.160
The, uh, family crest.

04:12.160 --> 04:14.160
Been to Russia, too.

04:14.160 --> 04:15.160
Samovar.

04:15.160 --> 04:17.160
A nice pair of icons.

04:17.160 --> 04:19.160
You know, it was beginning to rain when we came in here.

04:19.160 --> 04:22.160
I hope that chap's had the decency not to get my hat absolutely soaked.

04:23.160 --> 04:24.160
Hmm, Watson.

04:24.160 --> 04:26.160
Ah, I see he composes, too.

04:26.160 --> 04:28.160
Or someone does.

04:32.160 --> 04:34.160
Holmes, what do you think you're doing?

04:34.160 --> 04:38.160
You just can't start barging into a chap's house and start playing his piano?

04:38.160 --> 04:40.160
The violin is really my instrument, Watson.

04:40.160 --> 04:44.160
But I have a certain facility with the piano, even if I do say so.

04:44.160 --> 04:46.160
Do you know what this piece is called?

04:46.160 --> 04:47.160
No, I don't.

04:47.160 --> 04:48.160
I couldn't care less.

04:48.160 --> 04:50.160
Will you stop playing that thing?

04:50.160 --> 04:51.160
The Spider's Web.

04:51.160 --> 04:53.160
A ballet, apparently.

04:53.160 --> 04:56.160
And at this point, the spider attacks the fly.

05:03.160 --> 05:06.160
If you gentlemen wish to see my husband, I'm Mrs. Chelmson.

05:06.160 --> 05:07.160
How do you do?

05:07.160 --> 05:08.160
I'm Dr. Watson.

05:08.160 --> 05:10.160
This is my friend, Mr. Holmes.

05:10.160 --> 05:13.160
Oh, Mr. Sherlock Holmes.

05:14.160 --> 05:16.160
Madam, I've come here to return your husband's hat.

05:16.160 --> 05:18.160
He left it in our flat this evening.

05:18.160 --> 05:20.160
I thank you.

05:20.160 --> 05:22.160
I'm afraid my husband isn't home, Dr. Watson.

05:22.160 --> 05:27.160
Well, I wonder if you'd be good enough to ask him if, at his earliest convenience, he could return mine.

05:27.160 --> 05:29.160
My husband has other hats.

05:29.160 --> 05:30.160
Perhaps you...

05:30.160 --> 05:31.160
Oh, no, no.

05:31.160 --> 05:32.160
Thank you very much indeed.

05:32.160 --> 05:35.160
I'm very partial to my own.

05:35.160 --> 05:39.160
Well, I'm sorry to have disturbed you, Mrs. Chelmson.

05:45.160 --> 05:46.160
Mr. Holmes?

05:46.160 --> 05:48.160
Yes, madam?

05:48.160 --> 05:53.160
May I ask, did my husband go to your flat to consult you professionally?

05:53.160 --> 05:54.160
No, no.

05:54.160 --> 05:58.160
He merely came to return Dr. Watson's coat, which he had also taken by mistake.

05:58.160 --> 06:03.160
Then, Mr. Holmes, I would like to consult you professionally.

06:03.160 --> 06:07.160
My services are always available to anyone who has genuine need of them.

06:07.160 --> 06:09.160
My husband is in trouble.

06:09.160 --> 06:11.160
He gave us that impression.

06:11.160 --> 06:14.160
He's being blackmailed.

06:14.160 --> 06:16.160
By whom?

06:16.160 --> 06:23.160
Six months ago, we returned to London from St. Petersburg, where my husband was military attaché.

06:23.160 --> 06:27.160
During his stay in St. Petersburg, he met a ballerina named Olga Yaplanoff.

06:27.160 --> 06:29.160
Yaplanoff.

06:29.160 --> 06:33.160
Oh, yes, the premier dancers with the St. Petersburg Imperial Ballet.

06:33.160 --> 06:34.160
Yes.

06:34.160 --> 06:36.160
The troupe is playing in London now.

06:36.160 --> 06:37.160
They've been here all week.

06:37.160 --> 06:41.160
When my husband met Yaplanoff in Russia, they became friendly.

06:41.160 --> 06:43.160
Or it was nothing more than friendship.

06:43.160 --> 06:48.160
You see, my husband is an amateur composer, and their friendship was based on mutual love of music.

06:48.160 --> 06:50.160
I see.

06:50.160 --> 06:57.160
During their friendship, my husband disclosed some military secrets unthinkingly to Yaplanoff.

06:57.160 --> 07:01.160
She demanded 5,000 pounds, but then he was transferred to London.

07:01.160 --> 07:03.160
Now she's renewed her demands.

07:03.160 --> 07:06.160
Your husband has told you of this?

07:06.160 --> 07:11.160
Only of the original demand in St. Petersburg, but I know that she's renewed it here in London.

07:11.160 --> 07:13.160
Do you expect him back this evening?

07:13.160 --> 07:18.160
I don't know. His whole routine has been upset.

07:18.160 --> 07:21.160
Mr. Holmes, you must help.

07:21.160 --> 07:24.160
Madam, I detest blackmail in any form.

07:24.160 --> 07:28.160
You'll hear from me tomorrow.

07:28.160 --> 07:32.160
Oh, by the way, what was the dancer's name?

07:32.160 --> 07:34.160
Yaplanoff.

07:34.160 --> 07:48.160
Oh, yes. I wonder, would you mind writing it down for me?

07:48.160 --> 07:53.160
Thank you, madam.

07:53.160 --> 07:58.160
Oh, madam, about my hat, I wonder...

07:58.160 --> 08:00.160
Yes, Dr. Watson?

08:00.160 --> 08:23.160
Oh, well, no, it doesn't matter.

08:23.160 --> 08:26.160
Did you knock on the door or was it a knock?

08:26.160 --> 08:29.160
I am inside, Watson, not outside.

08:29.160 --> 08:31.160
Well, it must have been somebody else.

08:31.160 --> 08:38.160
It wasn't me. I'm inside, too.

08:38.160 --> 08:40.160
Ah, Inspector.

08:40.160 --> 08:43.160
Rather late for Scotland Yard to be out and about, isn't it?

08:43.160 --> 08:47.160
I'm sorry to get you out of bed at this time of night, Mr. Holmes.

08:47.160 --> 08:49.160
It's not a very pleasant business I've come about, either.

08:49.160 --> 08:52.160
Oh, look, I say, my hat.

08:52.160 --> 08:53.160
Dr. Watson.

08:53.160 --> 08:55.160
Holmes, look at this chance dung to it.

08:55.160 --> 08:57.160
It's absolutely soaked.

08:57.160 --> 09:00.160
Dr. Watson, do you wish to make a statement?

09:00.160 --> 09:06.160
You're not obliged to, but I must warn you that anything you say will be taken down and may be used as evidence.

09:06.160 --> 09:09.160
I won't half give this chap a piece of my mind when I catch up with him.

09:09.160 --> 09:13.160
I really... What? What did you... Who do you want to get...

09:13.160 --> 09:16.160
Holmes, what's he talking about?

09:16.160 --> 09:22.160
Dr. Watson, I must question you in connection with the murder of the Honorable Harry Shelton.

09:22.160 --> 09:26.160
Murder? I'll murder him when I catch up with him.

09:26.160 --> 09:28.160
What? What?

09:28.160 --> 09:35.160
Mr. Shelton was found murdered in St. James's Park, and your hat, Dr. Watson, was found beside the body.

09:37.160 --> 09:41.160
Well, an embarrassing predicament, eh, Watson? Can you explain it?

09:41.160 --> 09:44.160
Well, what do you mean, can I explain it?

09:44.160 --> 09:49.160
Well, you know perfectly well how Shelton got my hat. Well, tell the man. Tell him.

09:49.160 --> 09:51.160
Mr. Trey, how was Shelton murdered?

09:51.160 --> 09:54.160
Stabbed, Mr. Holmes.

09:54.160 --> 09:58.160
In the back? And we haven't been able to find a weapon yet.

09:58.160 --> 10:02.160
Any other suspects? I mean, apart from my old friend Watson here?

10:02.160 --> 10:07.160
Well, no. Less footpads did it.

10:07.160 --> 10:11.160
I'm sorry, Dr. Watson. That's evidence, you know.

10:11.160 --> 10:18.160
Holmes, will you kindly tell the strayed how my hat got mixed up in this affair?

10:18.160 --> 10:21.160
Patience, Watson. Patience. Now go get yourself dressed.

10:21.160 --> 10:25.160
We're going to St. James's Park, if you don't mind waiting a moment, Mr. Trey.

10:26.160 --> 10:31.160
Oh, uh, officer, you might keep an eye on Dr. Watson while he dresses.

10:31.160 --> 10:34.160
You never know, he may try and get out through the window.

10:37.160 --> 10:42.160
Well, I... I... This is a fine example of British justice.

10:42.160 --> 10:47.160
A fellow comes and pinches your hat, and you accuse me of murdering him to get it back.

10:47.160 --> 10:51.160
Hmph! I've never heard of such a thing in my life.

10:51.160 --> 10:56.160
I think of all the cups of tea you've had free without it. It makes me absolutely...

11:01.160 --> 11:05.160
Well, um, the body was lying about here, Mr. Holmes.

11:05.160 --> 11:07.160
I see. You removed it immediately?

11:07.160 --> 11:09.160
Well, almost immediately.

11:09.160 --> 11:12.160
A crowd of bodies lying about St. James's Park, you know.

11:12.160 --> 11:14.160
Anyhow, it was raining.

11:14.160 --> 11:19.160
Yes, of course, and the poor fellow may have taken cold, all the most considerate of you, Lestrade.

11:19.160 --> 11:22.160
It doesn't matter what evidence you may have destroyed.

11:22.160 --> 11:26.160
Well, from what you tell me on the way up here, things are beginning to take shape.

11:26.160 --> 11:28.160
You finally getting this overcoat?

11:28.160 --> 11:31.160
No, nothing. Only that knelt you were talking about.

11:31.160 --> 11:34.160
You know, the one with the twelve heels with the broken feet?

11:34.160 --> 11:36.160
Oh, that doesn't mean anything, I'm sure.

11:36.160 --> 11:38.160
No, I suppose not, Lestrade.

11:38.160 --> 11:41.160
And you were saying that things were beginning to shape up, eh?

11:41.160 --> 11:46.160
Oh, yes. Well, you see, Chelton met this woman in St. Petersburg.

11:46.160 --> 11:50.160
She comes to London with the ballet, and she wants to renew the friendship.

11:50.160 --> 11:55.160
He doesn't. So she lures him in here and stabs him.

11:55.160 --> 11:59.160
Very excitable, these Russian women. Take things like that seriously, you know.

11:59.160 --> 12:03.160
So all you have to do is place her here in the park, eh, Lestrade?

12:03.160 --> 12:04.160
Exactly.

12:04.160 --> 12:07.160
You have all the evidence you need that she was here?

12:07.160 --> 12:08.160
I have.

12:08.160 --> 12:12.160
The note, Lestrade. It tells Chelton to meet her here tonight.

12:12.160 --> 12:15.160
Well, I don't see it.

12:16.160 --> 12:20.160
Twelve heroes with broken feet.

12:20.160 --> 12:24.160
What have twelve heroes with broken feet to do with her meeting him here tonight?

12:24.160 --> 12:30.160
The numeral twelve, Lestrade, obviously refers to twelve o'clock midnight.

12:30.160 --> 12:32.160
Well, what about the heroes with the broken feet?

12:32.160 --> 12:35.160
A slight confusion on the ladies' part.

12:35.160 --> 12:38.160
Probably the influence of Cockney stagehands.

12:38.160 --> 12:40.160
She added an H where there shouldn't have been one.

12:40.160 --> 12:41.160
What?

12:41.160 --> 12:46.160
Then spell it as she spelled it, but without the H.

12:47.160 --> 12:50.160
E-R-O-S.

12:50.160 --> 12:53.160
Precisely. And here he is.

12:53.160 --> 12:57.160
Cupid, the Greek god Eros, with his broken feet.

12:59.160 --> 13:04.160
Oh, I see. Oh, then we have our murderess.

13:04.160 --> 13:07.160
It would appear so, wouldn't it?

13:07.160 --> 13:13.160
And now that the case is solved, perhaps I can go home and soak my feet.

13:19.160 --> 13:22.160
No, no, I know nothing, nothing.

13:22.160 --> 13:24.160
Please leave me alone. Please, you will go.

13:24.160 --> 13:25.160
Madam, I assure you...

13:25.160 --> 13:27.160
Where is Sergei?

13:27.160 --> 13:30.160
Oh, Sergei, come quick, quick. Help me.

13:30.160 --> 13:32.160
They want to hang a bull in Petrushka.

13:32.160 --> 13:34.160
Where is Sergei?

13:34.160 --> 13:35.160
Madam, we...

13:35.160 --> 13:39.160
Go away, you're a bad man. Disappear. Please disappear.

13:41.160 --> 13:44.160
The Spider's Web. Chelton wrote it.

13:45.160 --> 13:53.160
Madam, perhaps you'll be good enough to write as I dictate.

13:54.160 --> 13:59.160
Twelve heroes with broken feet.

13:59.160 --> 14:02.160
No, I do not do it, I swear.

14:02.160 --> 14:06.160
He was dead already when I go to meet him.

14:07.160 --> 14:09.160
You wish to make a statement, madam?

14:09.160 --> 14:15.160
You're not obliged to do so, but I warn you that anything you say will be taken down and may be used in evidence.

14:15.160 --> 14:18.160
He's got that expression on the brain to know...

14:18.160 --> 14:22.160
I do nothing except dance. I do not kill. I am dancer.

14:22.160 --> 14:26.160
You do not believe me. Look, I show you.

14:26.160 --> 14:29.160
Madam, please. Please, madam, I implore you.

14:29.160 --> 14:31.160
Sergei, this is Achille Harry Chelton.

14:31.160 --> 14:35.160
And me, Yaklanov. Tell them I cannot kill you in a fly.

14:38.160 --> 14:39.160
Who are you?

14:39.160 --> 14:40.160
Well, I...

14:40.160 --> 14:42.160
How do you harm Mr. Yaklanov like this?

14:42.160 --> 14:47.160
I am Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard. And Madam Yaklanov...

14:47.160 --> 14:52.160
And I, and I, and Sergei Smirnov, the director of this St. Petersburg Imperial Barrack.

14:52.160 --> 14:56.160
Bazaar himself shall hear of this outrage.

14:56.160 --> 15:04.160
Then, Mr. Smirnov, perhaps you will be good enough to explain the exact relationship between Madam Yaklanov and Mr. Chelton.

15:04.160 --> 15:10.160
Madam appears to be, to say the least of it, incoherent.

15:14.160 --> 15:17.160
So you had an acquaintance with Chelton in London?

15:17.160 --> 15:20.160
Yes, Sergei. I saw him again.

15:20.160 --> 15:24.160
I meet Harry in St. Petersburg. He does not wish to be soldier boy, he tells me.

15:24.160 --> 15:27.160
He wished to make beautiful music like Tchaikovsky.

15:27.160 --> 15:32.160
He plays his music for me. I say, Harry, you must be better soldier boy than you are music maker.

15:32.160 --> 15:35.160
You must be. It is not possible to be worse.

15:36.160 --> 15:40.160
Such music, like noises in the ear. It was ridiculous.

15:40.160 --> 15:43.160
Yes. Why was he looking at you like that, madam?

15:43.160 --> 15:47.160
He has written ballets since he left St. Petersburg. The Spider's Web, he calls it.

15:47.160 --> 15:53.160
He wished me to dance the spider. Me, Yaklanov, a spider. It is crazy.

15:53.160 --> 15:57.160
And tonight you arranged to meet him in St. James' Park after the evening performance?

15:57.160 --> 16:01.160
Yes, to tell him it is finished. No more will I listen to his music.

16:01.160 --> 16:03.160
And did you meet him in the park?

16:03.160 --> 16:08.160
Yes, but I was a little late. It was very dark.

16:08.160 --> 16:12.160
I see Harry on the ground, dead.

16:13.160 --> 16:15.160
I see. You saw no weapon?

16:15.160 --> 16:19.160
No, no weapon. I am frightened. I run away.

16:28.160 --> 16:32.160
Not bad, Mr. Smirnov. It has certain merit.

16:32.160 --> 16:33.160
Merit?

16:33.160 --> 16:45.160
Oh, look at Bosby dance to noises like that. Yes, listen.

16:47.160 --> 16:50.160
At the beginning, even, it is all wrong.

16:50.160 --> 16:54.160
Look, it should go like this. In three-four time.

16:54.160 --> 16:58.160
One more time.

17:00.160 --> 17:06.160
Very pretty, Mr. Smirnov. But we happen to have a murder on our hands.

17:06.160 --> 17:10.160
Oh, madam, does this dagger belong to you?

17:10.160 --> 17:13.160
Yes, I kill myself with it.

17:13.160 --> 17:16.160
Oh, now, very, madam, there is no need to go that far.

17:16.160 --> 17:21.160
I believe, Watson, the lady means she kills herself with it in the ballet.

17:21.160 --> 17:23.160
Yes, in the ballet.

17:23.160 --> 17:27.160
There are some interesting traces of blood on that, Estrella.

17:27.160 --> 17:30.160
No, no, it is not true.

17:32.160 --> 17:37.160
I am afraid, madam, I will have to ask you to accompany me to Scotland Yard for further questioning.

17:38.160 --> 17:41.160
You will go, Olga. It is only bluff.

17:41.160 --> 17:45.160
Yes, Sergei. I will dress.

17:45.160 --> 17:50.160
I will telegraph to Sartre. Himself. Personally.

17:50.160 --> 17:55.160
You will see. It may even be war. War!

18:00.160 --> 18:03.160
War!

18:03.160 --> 18:06.160
Man!

18:09.160 --> 18:12.160
Foreigners. Women.

18:12.160 --> 18:15.160
Nobody could be logical about them.

18:15.160 --> 18:19.160
Not even Sherlock Holmes.

18:20.160 --> 18:23.160
Holmes, can't you think, sitting down?

18:23.160 --> 18:26.160
This is the third time I have read this page already.

18:26.160 --> 18:28.160
Yakovlev's handwriting. That's what baffles me.

18:28.160 --> 18:30.160
The hand of a strong woman. And yet you saw her, Watson.

18:30.160 --> 18:34.160
No will of her own. Absolutely no will. It's illogical.

18:34.160 --> 18:37.160
Well, the inspector seemed to think the same thing.

18:37.160 --> 18:42.160
But he has built up a pretty logical case against her with your help.

18:42.160 --> 18:44.160
Perhaps too logical.

18:44.160 --> 18:48.160
Attack when you expect them to run and run when you expect them to attack.

18:48.160 --> 18:53.160
You know, Kelton wrote pretty bad music. That could be a motive for murder.

18:53.160 --> 18:55.160
I thought about it myself on occasion.

18:55.160 --> 18:59.160
But this handwriting, Watson. If only I could get to the bottom of it.

19:00.160 --> 19:05.160
I say, Holmes, do you think they could let me have my hat back before the trial?

19:05.160 --> 19:08.160
I didn't think my drag on for days.

19:08.160 --> 19:11.160
Lestrade keeps on saying it's evidence.

19:11.160 --> 19:13.160
Nope. There's only one thing to do.

19:13.160 --> 19:17.160
Have Lestrade confront Sergei Smirnov with Mrs. Kelton.

19:17.160 --> 19:18.160
Why?

19:18.160 --> 19:22.160
Because it's perfectly obvious why and by whom Kelton was killed.

19:22.160 --> 19:27.160
The only thing I can't get to the bottom of is this mysterious business of the handwriting.

19:28.160 --> 19:31.160
Do you know who murdered Kelton then?

19:31.160 --> 19:32.160
Yes, of course I do.

19:32.160 --> 19:34.160
Don't you?

19:34.160 --> 19:35.160
No.

19:44.160 --> 19:47.160
Then you never met Sergei Smirnov before, Mrs. Kelton.

19:47.160 --> 19:49.160
Either in St. Petersburg or London.

19:49.160 --> 19:51.160
I know, Mr. Holmes.

19:51.160 --> 19:55.160
I've thought Mr. Smirnov for long as you ask me to, Mr. Holmes.

19:55.160 --> 19:56.160
Oh, good.

19:56.160 --> 19:57.160
Ah!

19:57.160 --> 19:58.160
Mr. Brassier!

19:58.160 --> 19:59.160
I beg your pardon.

19:59.160 --> 20:01.160
I am furious!

20:01.160 --> 20:03.160
Right in the midst of rehearsal!

20:03.160 --> 20:04.160
I am dragged away!

20:04.160 --> 20:08.160
Well, I'm sure the good inspector apologized most profusely to Smirnov.

20:08.160 --> 20:10.160
I don't believe you've met Mrs. Kelton.

20:10.160 --> 20:11.160
Hmm?

20:11.160 --> 20:12.160
Kelton.

20:12.160 --> 20:13.160
Who?

20:13.160 --> 20:14.160
Mrs. Kelton.

20:14.160 --> 20:17.160
Ah! Murder!

20:17.160 --> 20:20.160
Murder!

20:29.160 --> 20:37.160
Then perhaps, Mr. Smirnov, you'd be good enough to play the introductory music to the Spiders' Web Ballet for Mrs. Kelton, as you did for us last night.

20:37.160 --> 20:38.160
Eh?

20:38.160 --> 20:48.160
Didn't it strike you as curious, Lestrade, that Mr. Smirnov should know Kelton's ballet music, which wasn't composed until after Kelton left St. Petersburg?

20:48.160 --> 20:50.160
Well, I...

20:50.160 --> 20:51.160
Ah.

20:51.160 --> 20:53.160
Yakovov didn't show it to him.

20:53.160 --> 20:55.160
She did everything she could to hide their meetings.

20:55.160 --> 20:59.160
That left only one place where he could have learned that music.

21:00.160 --> 21:01.160
Here.

21:01.160 --> 21:03.160
In this house.

21:03.160 --> 21:05.160
At that piano.

21:05.160 --> 21:08.160
In Kelton's absence, naturally.

21:08.160 --> 21:11.160
I don't follow you, Holmes.

21:11.160 --> 21:23.160
The budding affair of the Heartless Trade was not, as Mrs. Kelton would have us believe, between her husband and Mademoiselle Yakovov, but between herself and Mr. Smirnov.

21:24.160 --> 21:27.160
Between Smirnov and Mrs. Kelton?

21:27.160 --> 21:29.160
Ridiculous.

21:29.160 --> 21:32.160
Then how did you learn Kelton's ballet?

21:32.160 --> 21:34.160
I...

21:34.160 --> 21:36.160
I...

21:36.160 --> 21:38.160
You wish to make a statement, sir?

21:38.160 --> 21:43.160
You're not obliged to do so, but I warn you that anything you say will be taken down and maybe...

21:43.160 --> 21:45.160
Are these the shoes you meant, Holmes?

21:45.160 --> 21:47.160
I believe so, Watson.

21:47.160 --> 21:53.160
Mrs. Kelton, did you by any chance go out last night after Dr. Watson and I departed?

21:53.160 --> 21:55.160
No, I didn't.

21:56.160 --> 21:59.160
I mean, no, I didn't.

21:59.160 --> 22:01.160
Yet these are the shoes you were wearing.

22:01.160 --> 22:08.160
If you didn't go out, how do you account for the mud on the soles and the blades of grass?

22:08.160 --> 22:12.160
It didn't start to rain until after we left.

22:14.160 --> 22:18.160
Mr. Holmes, I don't think it's necessary that I explain anything.

22:18.160 --> 22:20.160
You...

22:20.160 --> 22:24.160
Do you mean she did it or he did it or both?

22:24.160 --> 22:27.160
Who are you accusing of murder, Holmes?

22:27.160 --> 22:31.160
Mrs. Kelton, alone.

22:31.160 --> 22:33.160
Ah, ah.

22:33.160 --> 22:36.160
So you killed for love of me?

22:36.160 --> 22:40.160
And accomplished the double task of placing the guilt on Mademoiselle Yaxonov.

22:40.160 --> 22:44.160
You undoubtedly found her note in your husband's pocket.

22:44.160 --> 22:46.160
Ah!

22:46.160 --> 22:49.160
To kill for love.

22:49.160 --> 22:51.160
I can understand.

22:51.160 --> 22:56.160
But to blame poor little Olga.

22:56.160 --> 22:59.160
That I will never forgive.

22:59.160 --> 23:01.160
Who would replace her in a ballet?

23:01.160 --> 23:07.160
You seem to forget, Mr. Holmes, that the dagger was found in Mademoiselle Yaxonov's room.

23:07.160 --> 23:09.160
Ah, yes, the dagger.

23:09.160 --> 23:12.160
When did Mrs. Kelton have the dagger, Mr. Smirnoff?

23:12.160 --> 23:14.160
Three days ago.

23:14.160 --> 23:17.160
I forget to leave it here after the ballet.

23:17.160 --> 23:23.160
The next day, Elaine returned it to me with a few drops of her own blood on it.

23:23.160 --> 23:27.160
Holmes, are you sure this time?

23:27.160 --> 23:30.160
I was almost sure from the beginning, Mr. Traid.

23:30.160 --> 23:33.160
I just couldn't explain the handwriting and the temperament.

23:33.160 --> 23:35.160
And there really wasn't a logical explanation.

23:35.160 --> 23:38.160
I mean the strong-minded woman with the weak handwriting.

23:38.160 --> 23:41.160
And the weak-minded woman with the strong handwriting.

23:41.160 --> 23:43.160
The inconsistency of the female, Watson.

23:43.160 --> 23:49.160
Yet she was clever in engaging me to make sure that Mademoiselle Yaxonov would be suspected.

23:49.160 --> 23:53.160
Mrs. Kelton, I must ask you to come with me to Scotland Yard.

23:53.160 --> 23:55.160
Do you wish to make a statement?

23:58.160 --> 24:04.160
You're not obliged to, but I warn you that anything you say will be taken down and may be used in evidence.

24:04.160 --> 24:07.160
I must go and free poor little Olga from prison.

24:07.160 --> 24:09.160
She has suffered so.

24:12.160 --> 24:14.160
She'll dance so much better now.

24:14.160 --> 24:15.160
Oh!

24:20.160 --> 24:24.160
You know, I'm afraid I'm going to have to leave you to it.

24:24.160 --> 24:27.160
I'm afraid I'm going to have to leave you to it.

24:27.160 --> 24:30.160
I'm afraid I'm going to have to leave you to it.

24:30.160 --> 24:39.160
You mean, Holmes, that you knew Mrs. Kelton had committed the murder, but the one thing that confused you was her handwriting?

24:39.160 --> 24:41.160
Her handwriting and character, Watson.

24:41.160 --> 24:45.160
The evidence was logical, but the personalities were not.

24:45.160 --> 24:48.160
Well, they were logical enough in their way, Holmes, or were they just feminine?

24:48.160 --> 24:50.160
Yes, that's it, Watson.

24:50.160 --> 24:52.160
They were just feminine.

24:52.160 --> 24:55.160
I must devote more time to that, Watson.

24:55.160 --> 25:00.160
I must devote more time to that.

25:55.160 --> 26:01.160
THE END

